# Project Summary

This project explores how personality traits relate to the use of psychoactive substances like cannabis, ecstasy, cocaine, and others, using a real-world behavioral dataset of over 1,800 individuals. The goal was to see whether people who have used these substances show distinct personality patterns compared to those who haven’t.

To do this, I used a dataset that includes scores from the NEO Five-Factor Inventory (covering traits like neuroticism, openness, agreeableness, etc.), as well as impulsivity and sensation-seeking scales. I converted the drug use data into binary categories—people who had used at least one substance versus those who hadn’t—and ran statistical comparisons using t-tests.

The results were pretty clear: users tended to score higher in **neuroticism**, **openness**, **impulsivity**, and **sensation seeking**, and lower in **agreeableness** and **conscientiousness**. Interestingly, **extraversion didn’t differ much** between the groups. These patterns are consistent with established psychological research, suggesting that emotional reactivity and thrill-seeking may be linked to experimentation with drugs.

The included bar plot shows average trait scores for both groups side by side, which visually backs up the statistical differences found.

Overall, this project helped me practice data wrangling, statistical testing, and visualization in R, and gave me insight into how behavioral traits may influence real-world decisions like substance use.
